cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this benchmark or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "When should an RL execution policy be trusted? A pre-registered synthetic-market study of regret-calibrated selective intervention under distribution shift"
authors:
  - family-names: Sakthivel
    given-names: Manas
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-10-02
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  A pre-registered synthetic-market study of whether an uncertainty-based gate can decide,
  at order arrival, when to trust a reinforcement-learning execution policy and when to
  fall back to TWAP. The central claim was not supported: the gate's score tracks
  counterfactual regret only weakly (Spearman rho +0.108, AUROC 0.542), and the gate
  improves on always-RL by -0.013 bp against a pre-registered 0.25 bp threshold. All data
  are synthetic.
keywords:
  - optimal execution
  - reinforcement learning
  - distribution shift
  - selective prediction
  - learning to defer
  - pre-registration
repository-code: "https://github.com/ManasSakthivel/execshift"
